<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhou dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1046–256 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zhou dynasty was the longest-ruling royal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which overthrew the Shang around 1046 BCE and nominally ruled until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> ended it in 256 BCE. Its kings granted lands to kinsmen and allies as hereditary states and justified their rule by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>. After the western capital fell in 771 BCE, the Eastern Zhou kings lost real power to the states of the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zhou dynasty" width="318" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhou_dynasty_1000_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The eastern court extracted around 100,000 rolls of silk annually from the Northern <b>Zhou dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Qi was founded shortly after the <b>Zhou</b> conquest of the Shang, conventionally dated to 1046 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The interruption of the Tang line by Empress Wu Zetian (r. 690–705), who proclaimed her own <b>Zhou dynasty</b>, was followed by the long reign of Emperor Xuanzong (r. 712–756), whose Kaiyuan era (713–741) is regarded by Chinese historians as the Tang's cultural and administrative zenith.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Zhou dynasty</b>, which overthrew the Shang around 1046 BCE, long inscriptions were cast in bronze ritual vessels, recording royal gifts, appointments and victories; the longest has nearly five hundred characters.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Neuk-do Island shell midden, of the Late Mumun and Early Iron Age, held a few iron objects and Lelang and Yayoi pottery, showing that local societies were drawn into closer contact with the Late <b>Zhou dynasty</b>, the Final Jōmon and the Early Yayoi.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mumun-culture">Mumun culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The establishment of the Eastern <b>Zhou dynasty</b> in 770 BCE began a gradual decline of the Chinese political order.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
